  "notes": "West leads the ♠J against 3NT, won by East with the ♠A. East switches to the ♣Q. South has more than enough winners for his contract (2 spades, 2 hearts, and 6 diamonds). His only concern is that the defense may be able to take 4 club tricks to go with their ♠A.\n\nIf South covers the ♣Q with the ♣K, the contract might fail. On the above layout, for example, West will win the ♣A and return the suit to East's ♣J. A further club lead from East will sink the contract.\n\nSouth can ensure the contract by ducking the first round of clubs. Whatever club East continues, South covers as cheaply as possible. West can win this trick, but South's remaining club holding will act as a stopper as long as West is on lead.",
